    {
      "id": 85607,
      "name": "Silverleaf",
      "username": "Silverleaf",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/{size}/2332_2.png",
      "created_at": "2014-01-08T08:31:39.000Z",
      "cooked": "<p>Names, like language, evolve. New ones are constantly being made up, old ones rediscovered or modified, being created from pet names or whatever.</p><p>As a family historian, I'd certainly have been grateful if my ancestors had been a touch more imaginative with their naming rather than churning out Johns and Williams and Elizabeths and Marys. Try finding records of \"your\" John Roberts born in Shropshire in about 1850, and his father with the same name... I was so grateful when I found a Sylvanus Dunn!</p><p>And speaking of ancestors, I'm fairly sure mine didn't have \"more spare time\". They worked long shifts in very physical jobs like mining, agricultural labouring, cotton spinning/weaving, tool grinding/cutlery, and they had a lot of children to take care of on very little money, like a good number of people at the time.&nbsp;</p>",
      "post_number": 16,
