      "user_title": null,
      "bookmarked": false,
      "raw": "<p>Names, like language, evolve. New ones are constantly being made up, old ones rediscovered or modified, being created from pet names or whatever.</p><p>As a family historian, I&#39;d certainly have been grateful if my ancestors had been a touch more imaginative with their naming rather than churning out Johns and Williams and Elizabeths and Marys. Try finding records of &quot;your&quot; John Roberts born in Shropshire in about 1850, and his father with the same name... I was so grateful when I found a Sylvanus Dunn!</p><p>And speaking of ancestors, I&#39;m fairly sure mine didn&#39;t have &quot;more spare time&quot;. They worked long shifts in very physical jobs like mining, agricultural labouring, cotton spinning/weaving, tool grinding/cutlery, and they had a lot of children to take care of on very little money, like a good number of people at the time.&nbsp;</p>",
